What should happen when another request arrives before the current one finishes? The four
higher-order mapping operators answer that question in four different ways:

- {% highlight %}switchMap{% /highlight %}: switch to the latest.
- {% highlight %}concatMap{% /highlight %}: queue it and keep the order.
- {% highlight %}mergeMap{% /highlight %}: let operations overlap.
- {% highlight %}exhaustMap{% /highlight %}: ignore it while busy.

Same inputs, same durations. Only the operator changes.

## The experiment

Three events, three inner streams. Each event can start a cold request Observable; the operator
decides when, or whether, it is subscribed.

| Event | Arrives at | Request takes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| A | 0s | 5s |
| B | 1s | 3s |
| C | 2s | 1s |

Each request emits one response, then completes. There are no errors in this example, and every
timeline uses the same scale.

```ts
events$.pipe(operator((id) => request$(id)));
```

## {% highlight %}switchMap{% /highlight %}: follow the latest

A new event unsubscribes the previous inner stream.

{% diagram id="rxjs-mapping-operators/switchmap" alt="Timeline for switchMap: A starts at 0s and is cancelled at 1s, B starts at 1s and is cancelled at 2s, C starts at 2s and responds at 3s." caption="Bars are active subscriptions; Out shows the responses." /%}

A stops at 1s, B stops at 2s, and only C responds, at 3s. Use it for search results that must
follow the latest query. Unsubscribing does not undo server-side changes.

## {% highlight %}concatMap{% /highlight %}: wait your turn

New values wait in a queue; each starts after the previous one completes.

{% diagram id="rxjs-mapping-operators/concatmap" alt="Timeline for concatMap: A runs from 0s and responds at 5s; B waits in the queue and runs from 5s to 8s; C waits and runs from 8s to 9s." caption="The dotted lines are events waiting in the queue." /%}

A responds at 5s, then B at 8s, then C at 9s. Use it for ordered saves that must all run. If an
inner stream never completes, the queue stalls.

## {% highlight %}mergeMap{% /highlight %}: let work overlap

Subscribe as events arrive and forward responses as they arrive.

{% diagram id="rxjs-mapping-operators/mergemap" alt="Timeline for mergeMap: A, B and C start as they arrive and overlap; responses come out C at 3s, B at 4s and A at 5s." caption="All three requests run at once; responses arrive in the order they finish." /%}

Responses arrive C, B, A: input order is not preserved. Use it for independent requests that can
overlap. Concurrency is unlimited by default; pass a limit if you need one.

## {% highlight %}exhaustMap{% /highlight %}: busy? Ignore it

New events are ignored until the active inner stream completes.

{% diagram id="rxjs-mapping-operators/exhaustmap" alt="Timeline for exhaustMap: A starts at 0s and responds at 5s; B and C arrive while A is running and are ignored." caption="B and C are dropped, not queued." /%}

Only A starts. B and C are dropped, not queued. After A completes, a future event can start a new
request. Use it to ignore repeated clicks while a form is submitting.

## Choose the behaviour

| What should a new event do? | Operator | Responses |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Replace the current work | `switchMap` | C at 3s |
| Wait and preserve order | `concatMap` | A 5s, B 8s, C 9s |
| Run alongside current work | `mergeMap` | C 3s, B 4s, A 5s |
| Be ignored while busy | `exhaustMap` | A at 5s |

The operator encodes your concurrency policy: choose it from the behaviour your UI needs. These are
RxJS operators, also used inside NgRx effects.

Sources: the RxJS API for [switchMap](https://rxjs.dev/api/operators/switchMap), [concatMap](https://rxjs.dev/api/operators/concatMap), [mergeMap](https://rxjs.dev/api/operators/mergeMap) and [exhaustMap](https://rxjs.dev/api/operators/exhaustMap), and Angular's [HTTP client guide](https://angular.dev/guide/http).
